# - Nuclear Blast Effects
{6000}{}{NUCLEAR BLAST EFFECTS}
{6001}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6002}{}{Vaults are designed to provide protection from the }
{6003}{}{effects of a nuclear blast. To better understand the }
{6004}{}{protection provided, we have included a section from the }
{6005}{}{High Energy Weapons FAQ that explains how a nuclear }
{6006}{}{blast causes damage.}
{6007}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6008}{}{The first thing bomb victims experience is the intense }
{6009}{}{flux of photons from the blast, which releases 70-80% of }
{6010}{}{the bomb's energy. The effects go up to third degree }
{6011}{}{thermal burns and are not a pretty sight. Initial }
{6012}{}{deaths are due to this effect.}
{6013}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6014}{}{The next phenomenon is the supersonic blast front. You }
{6015}{}{see it before you hear it. The pressure front has the }
{6016}{}{effect of blowing away anything in its path.}
{6017}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6018}{}{After the front comes the overpressure phase. It would }
{6019}{}{feel like being underwater a few hundred meters. (At a }
{6020}{}{few thousand meters under the sea, pressurized hulls }
{6021}{}{implode.) The pressure gradually dies off, and there is }
{6022}{}{a negative overpressure phase, with a reversed blast }
{6023}{}{wind. This reversal is due to air rushing back to fill }
{6024}{}{the void left by the explosion.}
{6025}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6026}{}{The air gradually returns to normal atmospheric }
{6027}{}{pressure. At this stage, fires caused by electrical }
{6028}{}{destruction and ignited debris turn the place into a }
{6029}{}{firestorm.}
{6030}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6031}{}{Then come the middle term effects such as keloid }
{6032}{}{formation and retinal blastoma. Genetic or hereditary }
{6033}{}{damage can appear up to forty years after initial }
{6034}{}{irradiation.}
{6035}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6036}{}{ATMOSPHERIC EFFECTS OF BLASTS}
{6037}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6038}{}{- The Mushroom Cloud. The heat from fusion and }
{6039}{}{fission instantaneously raises the surrounding air to 10 }
{6040}{}{million degrees C. This superheated air plasma gives }
{6041}{}{off so much light that it looks brighter than the sun, }
{6042}{}{and is visible hundreds of kilometers (km) away. The }
{6043}{}{resultant fireball quickly expands. It is made up of hot }
{6044}{}{air, and hence rises at a rate of a few hundred meters }
{6045}{}{per second. After a minute or so, the fireball has risen }
{6046}{}{to a few kilometers, and has cooled off to the extent }
{6047}{}{that it no longer radiates.}
{6048}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6049}{}{The surrounding cooler air exerts some drag on this }
{6050}{}{rising air, which slows down the outer edges of the }
{6051}{}{cloud. The unimpeded inner portion rises a bit quicker }
{6052}{}{than the outer edges. A vacuum effect occurs when the }
{6053}{}{outer portion occupies the vacuum left by the higher }
{6054}{}{inner portion. The result is a smoke ring.}
{6055}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6056}{}{The inner material gradually expands out into a }
{6057}{}{mushroom cloud, due to convection. If the explosion is }
{6058}{}{on the ground, dirt and radioactive debris get sucked up }
{6059}{}{the stem, which sits below the fireball.}
{6060}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6061}{}{Collisions and ionization of the cloud particles result }
{6062}{}{in lightning bolts flickering to the ground.}
{6063}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6064}{}{Initially, the cloud is orange-red due to a chemical }
{6065}{}{reaction when the air is heated. When the cloud cools}
{6066}{}{to air temperature, the water vapor starts to}
{6067}{}{condense. The cloud turns from red to white.}
{6068}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6069}{}{In the final stages, the cloud can get about 100km}
{6070}{}{across and 40km high, for a megaton class explosion.}
{6071}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6072}{}{- Electromagnetic Pulse (EMP). A nuclear explosion }
{6073}{}{gives off radiation at all wavelengths of light. Some is }
{6074}{}{in the radio/radar portion of the spectrum - the EMP }
{6075}{}{effect. The EMP effect increases the higher you go into }
{6076}{}{the atmosphere. High altitude explosions can knock out }
{6077}{}{electronics by inducing a current surge in closed }
{6078}{}{circuit metallic objects - electronics, power lines, }
{6079}{}{phone lines, TVs, radios, etc. The damage range can be }
{6080}{}{over 1000km.}
{6081}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6082}{}{OVERVIEW OF IMMEDIATE EFFECTS}
{6083}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6084}{}{The three categories of immediate effects are: blast, }
{6085}{}{thermal radiation (heat), and prompt ionizing or nuclear }
{6086}{}{radiation. Their relative importance varies with the }
{6087}{}{yield of the bomb. At low yields, all three can be }
{6088}{}{significant sources of injury. With an explosive yield }
{6089}{}{of about 2.5 kilotons (kT), the three effects are }
{6090}{}{roughly equal. All are capable of inflicting fatal }
{6091}{}{injuries at a range of 1 km.}
{6092}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6093}{}{The fraction of a bomb's yield emitted as thermal }
{6094}{}{radiation, blast, and ionizing radiation is essentially }
{6095}{}{constant for all yields, but the way the different forms }
{6096}{}{of energy interact with air and target vary }
{6097}{}{dramatically.}
{6098}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6099}{}{Air is essentially transparent to thermal radiation. The }
{6100}{}{thermal radiation affects exposed surfaces, producing }
{6101}{}{damage by rapid heating. A bomb that is 100 times }
{6102}{}{larger can produce equal thermal radiation intensities }
{6103}{}{over areas 100 times larger. The area of an (imaginary) }
{6104}{}{sphere centered on the explosion increases with the }
{6105}{}{square of the radius. Thus the destructive radius }
{6106}{}{increases with the square root of the yield (this is the }
{6107}{}{familiar inverse square law of electromagnetic }
{6108}{}{radiation). Actually the rate of increase is somewhat }
{6109}{}{less, partly due to the fact that larger bombs emit heat }
{6110}{}{more slowly which reduces the damage produced by }
{6111}{}{each calorie of heat. It is important to note that the }
{6112}{}{area subjected to damage by thermal radiation }
{6113}{}{increases almost linearly with yield.}
{6114}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6115}{}{Blast effect is a volume effect. The blast wave }
{6116}{}{deposits energy in the material it passes through, }
{6117}{}{including air. When the blast wave passes through solid }
{6118}{}{material, the energy left behind causes damage.}
{6119}{}{When it passes through air it simply grows weaker. The }
{6120}{}{more matter the energy travels through, the smaller the }
{6121}{}{effect. The amount of matter increases with the volume }
{6122}{}{of the imaginary sphere centered on the explosion. }
{6123}{}{Blast effects thus scale with the inverse cube law}
{6124}{}{which relates radius to volume.}
{6125}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6126}{}{The intensity of nuclear radiation decreases with the }
{6127}{}{inverse square law like thermal radiation. However }
{6128}{}{nuclear radiation is also strongly absorbed by the air }
{6129}{}{it travels through, which causes the intensity to drop }
{6130}{}{off much more rapidly.}
{6131}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6132}{}{These scaling laws show that the effects of thermal }
{6133}{}{radiation grow rapidly with yield (relative to blast), }
{6134}{}{while those of radiation rapidly decline.}
{6135}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6136}{}{In a small nuclear attack (bomb yield approx. 15kT) }
{6137}{}{casualties (including fatalities) would be seen from all }
{6138}{}{three causes. Burns (including those caused by an }
{6139}{}{ensuing fire storm) would be the most prevalent serious }
{6140}{}{injury (two thirds of those who would die the first}
{6141}{}{day would be burn victims), and occur at the greatest }
{6142}{}{range. Blast and burn injuries would be found in 60-70% }
{6143}{}{of all survivors. People close enough to suffer }
{6144}{}{significant radiation illness would be well inside the }
{6145}{}{lethal effects radius for blast and flash burns, as a }
{6146}{}{result only 30% of injured survivors would show }
{6147}{}{radiation illness. Many of those people would be }
{6148}{}{sheltered from burns and blast and thus escape the }
{6149}{}{main effects. Even so, most victims with radiation }
{6150}{}{illness would also have blast injuries or burns as well.}
{6151}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6152}{}{With yields in the range of hundreds of kilotons or }
{6153}{}{greater (typical for strategic warheads) immediate }
{6154}{}{radiation injury becomes insignificant. Dangerous }
{6155}{}{radiation levels only exist so close to the explosion }
{6156}{}{that surviving the blast is impossible. On the other }
{6157}{}{hand, fatal burns can be inflicted well beyond the range }
{6158}{}{of substantial blast damage. A 20 megaton bomb can }
{6159}{}{cause potentially fatal third degree burns at a range of }
{6160}{}{40km, where the blast can do little more than break }
{6161}{}{windows and cause superficial cuts.}
{6162}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6163}{}{A convenient rule of thumb for estimating the short-}
{6164}{}{term fatalities from all causes due to a nuclear attack }
{6165}{}{is to count everyone inside the 5 psi blast overpressure }
{6166}{}{contour around the hypocenter as a fatality. In reality, }
{6167}{}{substantial numbers of people inside the contour will}
{6168}{}{survive and substantial numbers outside the contour }
{6169}{}{will die, but the assumption is that these two groups }
{6170}{}{will be roughly equal in size and balance out. This }
{6171}{}{completely ignores any possible fallout effects.}
{6172}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6173}{}{OVERVIEW OF DELAYED EFFECTS}
{6174}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6175}{}{-Radioactive Contamination. The chief delayed effect is}
{6176}{}{the creation of huge amounts of radioactive material }
{6177}{}{with long lifetimes (half-lives ranging from days to }
{6178}{}{millennia). The primary source of these products is the }
{6179}{}{debris left from fission reactions. A potentially }
{6180}{}{significant secondary source is neutron capture by}
{6181}{}{non-radioactive isotopes both within the bomb and in }
{6182}{}{the outside environment.}
{6183}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6184}{}{When atoms fission they can split in some 40 different }
{6185}{}{ways, producing a mix of about 80 different isotopes. }
{6186}{}{These isotopes vary widely in stability; some are }
{6187}{}{completely stable while others undergo radioactive }
{6188}{}{decay with half-lives of fractions of a second. The}
{6189}{}{decaying isotopes may themselves form stable or }
{6190}{}{unstable daughter isotopes. The mixture thus quickly }
{6191}{}{becomes even more complex, some 300 different }
{6192}{}{isotopes of 36 elements have been identified in fission }
{6193}{}{products. }
{6194}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6195}{}{Short-lived isotopes release their decay energy rapidly, }
{6196}{}{creating intense radiation fields that also decline }
{6197}{}{quickly. Long-lived isotopes release energy over long }
{6198}{}{periods of time, creating radiation that is much less }
{6199}{}{intense but more persistent. Fission products thus }
{6200}{}{initially have a very high level of radiation that }
{6201}{}{declines quickly, but as the intensity of radiation }
{6202}{}{drops, so does the rate of decline.}
{6203}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6204}{}{A useful rule-of-thumb is the "rule of sevens". This }
{6205}{}{rule states that for every seven-fold increase in time }
{6206}{}{following a fission detonation (starting at or after 1 }
{6207}{}{hour), the radiation intensity decreases by a factor of }
{6208}{}{10. Thus after 7 hours, the residual fission}
{6209}{}{radioactivity declines 90%, to one-tenth its level of 1 }
{6210}{}{hour. After 7*7 hours (49 hours, approx. 2 days), the }
{6211}{}{level drops again by 90%. After 7*2 days (2 weeks) it }
{6212}{}{drops a further 90%; and so on for 14 weeks. The rule is }
{6213}{}{accurate to 25% for the first two weeks, and is accurate }
{6214}{}{to a factor of two for the first six months. After 6}
{6215}{}{months, the rate of decline becomes much more rapid. }
{6216}{}{The rule of sevens corresponds to an approximate t^-}
{6217}{}{1.2 scaling relationship.}
{6218}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6219}{}{These radioactive products are most hazardous when }
{6220}{}{they settle to the ground as "fallout". The rate at }
{6221}{}{which fallout settles depends very strongly on the }
{6222}{}{altitude at which the explosion occurs, and to a lesser }
{6223}{}{extent on the size of the explosion.}
{6224}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6225}{}{If the explosion is a true air-burst (the fireball does }
{6226}{}{not touch the ground), when the vaporized radioactive }
{6227}{}{products cool enough to condense and solidify, they will }
{6228}{}{do so to form microscopic particles. These particles are }
{6229}{}{mostly lifted high into the atmosphere by the rising }
{6230}{}{fireball, although significant amounts are deposited in}
{6231}{}{the lower atmosphere by mixing that occurs due to }
{6232}{}{convective circulation within the fireball. The larger }
{6233}{}{the explosion, the higher and faster the fallout is }
{6234}{}{lofted, and the smaller the proportion that is deposited }
{6235}{}{in the lower atmosphere. For explosions with yields of }
{6236}{}{100kT or less, the fireball does not rise above the }
{6237}{}{troposphere where precipitation occurs. All of this }
{6238}{}{fallout will thus be brought to the ground by weather }
{6239}{}{processes within months at most (usually much faster). }
{6240}{}{In the megaton range, the fireball rises so high that it }
{6241}{}{enters the stratosphere. The stratosphere is dry, and no}
{6242}{}{weather processes exist there to bring fallout down }
{6243}{}{quickly. Small fallout particles will descend over a }
{6244}{}{period of months or years. Such long-delayed fallout }
{6245}{}{has lost most of its hazard by the time it comes down, }
{6246}{}{and will be distributed on a global scale. As yields}
{6247}{}{increase above 100kT, progressively more and more of }
{6248}{}{the total fallout is injected into the stratosphere.}
{6249}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6250}{}{An explosion closer to the ground (close enough for the }
{6251}{}{fireball to touch) sucks large amounts of dirt into the }
{6252}{}{fireball. The dirt usually does not vaporize, and if it }
{6253}{}{does, there is so much of it that it forms large }
{6254}{}{particles. The radioactive isotopes are deposited on }
{6255}{}{soil particles, which can fall quickly to earth. }
{6256}{}{Fallout is deposited over a time span of minutes to }
{6257}{}{days, creating downwind contamination both nearby and }
{6258}{}{thousands of kilometers away. The most intense }
{6259}{}{radiation is created by nearby fallout, because it is }
{6260}{}{more densely deposited, and because short-lived }
{6261}{}{isotopes haven't decayed yet. Weather conditions can }
{6262}{}{affect this considerably of course. In particular, }
{6263}{}{rainfall can "rain out" fallout to create very intense }
{6264}{}{localized concentrations. Both external exposure to }
{6265}{}{penetrating radiation, and internal exposure (ingestion }
{6266}{}{of radioactive material) pose serious health risks.}
{6267}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6268}{}{Explosions close to the ground that do not touch it can }
{6269}{}{still generate substantial hazards immediately below }
{6270}{}{the burst point by neutron-activation. Neutrons }
{6271}{}{absorbed by the soil can generate considerable }
{6272}{}{radiation for several hours.}
{6273}{}{**END-PAR**}
{6274}{}{The megaton class weapons have been largely retired, }
{6275}{}{being replaced with much smaller yield warheads. The }
{6276}{}{yield of a modern strategic warhead is, with few }
{6277}{}{exceptions, now typically in the range of 200-750 kT. }
{6278}{}{Recent work with sophisticated climate models has }
{6279}{}{shown that this reduction in yield results in a much }
{6280}{}{larger proportion of the fallout being deposited in the }
{6281}{}{lower atmosphere, and a much faster and more intense }
{6282}{}{deposition of fallout than had been assumed in studies }
{6283}{}{made during the sixties and seventies. The reduction in }
{6284}{}{aggregate strategic arsenal yield that occurred when }
{6285}{}{high yield weapons were retired in favor of more }
{6286}{}{numerous lower yield weapons has actually increased }
{6287}{}{the fallout risk.}
{6288}{}{**END-DISK**}
